  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King delivered 6.0 innings of one-run ball and the offense scored once. That's what happened last night.",
    "what_this_means": "King held St. Louis to one hit through six frames, struck out six, and gave the Padres every chance to build on an early lead they'd grabbed in the first inning. They never did. Four hits and nine strikeouts tells the full story. The opportunity existed; the execution didn't. They were tied after the sixth, trailing by one after the seventh, and out of outs by the ninth. A quality start from their right-hander meant nothing because the lineup couldn't manufacture a second run. This is the margin they're working with now: King gives them air, and they hand it back. One run is not a platform. One hit is not offense. The bats left the building.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what fragile looks like from the inside. The pitching has been steady enough to win games. King proved that again. But the offense is becoming a liability so consistent it's starting to feel structural. Nine strikeouts against one of St. Louis's starters is not a slump. It's a locked door. The pattern has not changed in five days; it's gotten sharper. They're 4-6 in their last ten, sitting one back in a division where one run is the difference between staying in it and falling out. The margin is real, and they're burning it on nights like this.",
